export const CA6={title:'Publicity and marketing',hook:'Publicity tells the story of your campaign to the whole community—and it is scored separately from your activities. The top score requires high-level exposure in four or more media sources with a realistic estimate of the audience. A front-page school newspaper story, a radio interview, a library display and a social media series can turn a good campaign into a well-known one.',objectives:['Plan publicity across four or more forms of media.','Write a press release and pitch to local media.','Estimate audience size realistically and with evidence.','Promote HOSA and your health science program professionally.'],sections:[
['Why publicity matters',[
'Publicity spreads your message beyond the people who attend events, builds **credibility** for your campaign and promotes **goodwill** for HOSA and your health science program—an explicit purpose of the event. It also earns points: the rating sheet scores **strength of publicity**, with full points for **four or more media sources** and a **realistic estimate** of audience.',
'Publicity is different from activities. A health fair booth is an activity; a newspaper article **about** the booth is publicity. A social media post can be both an original item and publicity if it promotes the campaign to a wide audience.',
'Plan publicity from the beginning so each activity is announced before and reported after.',
sort('Activity or publicity?',['Campaign activity','Publicity about the campaign'],[['Running a CPR workshop',0],['A newspaper article about the workshop',1],['Hosting a health fair booth',0],['A radio interview about the campaign',1],['A school announcement promoting the event',1],['A creek clean-up day',0]])]],
['Forms of media',[
'Aim for variety: **print** (school and local newspapers, newsletters, flyers, posters), **broadcast** (local radio and TV, school morning announcements), **digital** (social media posts, websites, email newsletters, partner websites), and **community displays** (library, clinic or store displays, bulletin boards, marquees).',
'Each form reaches different people. Older adults may read the local paper; teens see social media and school announcements; parents read school newsletters. Partners can share your content through their own channels—a hospital’s newsletter or a library’s social media—multiplying reach.',
'Keep a **publicity log**: outlet, date, format, link or copy and estimated audience.',
"Ask each outlet how it measures its audience before the story runs, so you can record accurate numbers right away.",
match('Media outlet and typical audience',[['School newspaper','Students and staff'],['Local radio','Commuters and community members'],['Partner hospital newsletter','Patients and families'],['Library display','Library visitors of all ages'],['Chapter social media','Followers and their networks'],['School marquee','Families driving by']])]],
['Writing a press release',[
'A **press release** gives media the facts in a ready-to-use format. Include a **headline**, **date and location**, an opening paragraph answering **who, what, when, where and why**, a **quote** from a team member or partner, details about how the public can participate and **contact information** (usually your advisor). Keep it to one page.',
'Send it to local newspapers, radio stations, community calendars and partner organizations about **one to two weeks** before an event, and follow up politely. After the event, send a short summary with photos (with permission) for possible coverage.',
'Even if media do not run your story, partner and school channels often will.',
ex('Press release opening','Write the first paragraph of a press release for an invented event.',['Headline: Local HOSA Students Invite Families to “Clean Out Your Cabinet” Day','Who: Lincoln High School HOSA chapter and Main Street Pharmacy','What: Free medication take-back and safe storage education','When and where: Saturday, March 14, 10 a.m.–2 p.m., Lincoln Public Library','Why: To prevent accidental poisonings and medication misuse'],'“Lincoln High School HOSA students and Main Street Pharmacy will host a free ‘Clean Out Your Cabinet’ medication take-back and safe storage event on Saturday, March 14, from 10 a.m. to 2 p.m. at Lincoln Public Library to help families prevent accidental poisonings” (invented for practice).')]],
['Social media strategy',[
'Use a **chapter or school account**, not personal accounts. Plan a **content calendar** with posts before, during and after each activity. Use a consistent **hashtag** and visual style, tag partners (with permission) and include a call to action in every post.',
'Track **reach** (views, impressions) and **engagement** (likes, shares, comments, saves). Take **dated screenshots** of analytics for the portfolio. Follow school policies and safe messaging guidelines, and get permission from anyone pictured.',
'Ask partners to share or repost your content—this is often the easiest way to multiply your audience.',
"Short videos and carousels tend to reach more people than text-only posts, but every format works better with a clear first line and a visual that matches your campaign’s style.",
order('Social media plan for one event',['Announce the event a week ahead','Post a reminder the day before','Share live updates during the event','Post a thank-you and results afterward','Tag partners with permission','Screenshot analytics with dates'])]],
['Estimating audience realistically',[
'The rubric asks for a **realistic estimation or account of audience**. Use evidence: **newspaper circulation** figures (ask the paper or check its media kit), **radio audience** estimates provided by the station, **social media analytics**, **attendance counts** from sign-in sheets and **school enrollment** for announcements.',
'Avoid inflating numbers. Do not count the whole population of a city because a flyer was posted there. Say how each estimate was calculated, such as “Morning announcements reach approximately 1,200 students (school enrollment).” Estimations are acceptable when exact numbers are unknown, but they must be **based on evidence**.',
'Present totals clearly in the portfolio, broken down by media source.',
sort('Realistic estimate?',['Realistic','Unrealistic'],[['Video views from analytics screenshot',0],['“Our poster reached the whole state”',1],['Newspaper circulation from the paper’s media kit',0],['Counting every resident of the city for one flyer',1],['Sign-in sheet attendance',0],['School enrollment for morning announcements',0]])]],
['Promoting HOSA professionally',[
'The campaign should promote **goodwill and public relations** for HOSA and your health science program. Mention your chapter and program in press releases, posts and presentations; wear HOSA attire at events (as allowed); and use HOSA branding according to its guidelines.',
'Be professional in every public message: accurate, respectful, free of errors and focused on community benefit. Thank partners and participants publicly.',
'Strong, positive publicity can recruit new HOSA members and strengthen your chapter’s reputation for years.',
chk('Which publicity choice best promotes HOSA professionally?',['A proofread press release naming the chapter, partners and community benefit','A post with typos and no context','An unapproved use of a partner’s logo','A message criticizing another school'],'Professional, accurate and positive.')]],
['Practice scenario',[
kase('Building publicity',{a:{text:'Your campaign has been promoted only on your chapter’s social media. (Scenario invented for practice.) How do you reach four forms of media?',choices:[['Add a school newspaper article, a local radio interview and a library display','b','Correct.',true],['Post more on the same account','b','That is still one form of media.',false]]},b:{text:'How do you estimate the radio audience?',choices:[['Ask the station for its typical listener estimate for that time slot and cite it','c','Correct.',true],['Guess a large number','c','Estimates must be evidence-based.',false]]},c:{text:'Your publicity log lists four outlets with dates, copies and evidence-based audience estimates.',end:true}})]]
],takeaways:['Publicity is scored separately from activities; top scores need four or more media sources and a realistic, evidence-based audience estimate; publicity also builds goodwill for HOSA.','Use print, broadcast, digital and community displays; write one-page press releases (who, what, when, where, why, quote, contact) sent 1–2 weeks ahead; plan social media on chapter accounts with a hashtag, calls to action and dated analytics.','Estimate audiences from circulation, station data, analytics, sign-ins and enrollment—never inflate; keep a publicity log and promote HOSA professionally.'],terms:[['Publicity','Media coverage and promotion that tells the community about a campaign.'],['Press release','A one-page announcement written for media outlets.'],['Reach','The number of people who see a message.']],mcqs:[q('How many media sources earn the top publicity score?','Four or more','One','Two','Three','Strength of publicity.'),q('Which is publicity rather than an activity?','A newspaper article about your booth','Running the booth','Teaching a workshop','Leading a clean-up','Coverage.'),q('What should a press release answer first?','Who, what, when, where and why','Only who','Only the budget','The judges’ names','Key facts.'),q('When should you send a press release?','One to two weeks before the event','The day after','Never','A year later','Lead time.'),q('Which is a realistic audience estimate?','Video views from an analytics screenshot','The whole state for one poster','Every resident of the city','A random large number','Evidence-based.'),q('Which account should post campaign content?','A chapter or school account','A personal account','An anonymous account','A partner’s account without permission','Professionalism.'),q('Engagement measures:','Likes, shares, comments and saves','Only budget','Team size','Printing costs','Interaction.'),q('Why mention HOSA in publicity?','The campaign should promote goodwill for HOSA and the health science program','It is not allowed','To make it longer','For no reason','Event purpose.')],frq:{prompt:'Create a publicity plan for an invented Community Awareness campaign that reaches four forms of media and includes realistic audience estimates.',rubric:[['Four media',['newspaper','radio','social media','display','newsletter','announcements','four']],['Press release',['press release','who','what','when','where','why','quote','contact']],['Estimates',['circulation','analytics','enrollment','sign-in','realistic','evidence']],['Professionalism',['hosa','chapter account','permission','log','thank','partners']]],model:'For our invented campaign on stroke awareness among older adults, our publicity plan uses five forms of media. First, we will send a one-page press release to the local newspaper two weeks before our first workshop, with a headline, the who, what, when, where and why, a quote from our hospital partner and our advisor’s contact information; if published, we will estimate the audience from the newspaper’s reported circulation. Second, we will request a short interview on the local radio station’s community segment and record the station’s listener estimate for that time slot. Third, the senior centers and hospital will feature our workshops in their newsletters, using each organization’s distribution numbers. Fourth, the library will host a stroke-signs display for a month, and we will use the library’s visitor count for that period. Fifth, our chapter’s social media account will run a series of captioned videos and infographics with the hashtag #KnowTheSignsOurTown, posting before, during and after each workshop, tagging partners with permission and saving dated analytics screenshots of views and shares. We will keep a publicity log listing each outlet, date, format, copy or link and evidence for each audience estimate, and we will never inflate numbers. Every message will be proofread, mention our HOSA chapter and health science program and thank our partners, building goodwill for HOSA in the community.'},sources:[RULES,CDC_COMM,CTB]};

export const CA7={title:'Measuring and documenting impact',hook:'At the end of the year, judges will ask two questions: Did your campaign accomplish its goals, and how do you know? Teams that measured from the start—with baselines, sign-ins, counts and surveys—can answer confidently. Teams that did not are left with “we think it went well.” This lesson shows how to collect evidence that proves impact.',objectives:['Distinguish outputs, outcomes and impact.','Measure progress toward SMART objectives.','Collect dated evidence of activities and participation.','Document evidence of every team member’s participation.'],sections:[
['Outputs, outcomes and impact',[
'**Outputs** count what the campaign did: number of events, people attending, flyers distributed, video views. **Outcomes** describe changes in the audience: knowledge gained, attitudes shifted, behaviors adopted, such as more people registering as organ donors or dropping off medications. **Impact** describes longer-term change in the community, such as fewer poisonings or cleaner water.',
'Judges value outcomes most because they show your campaign made a difference, but outputs show reach and effort. Most student campaigns can measure outputs and short-term outcomes; long-term impact may be described as expected or early evidence.',
'Match each SMART objective to a measure before the campaign starts.',
sort('Output, outcome or impact?',['Output','Outcome','Impact'],[['300 flyers distributed',0],['80 students signed up as organ donors',1],['Fewer accidental poisonings in the county over years',2],['150 people attended workshops',0],['Quiz scores rose from 40% to 85%',1],['A cleaner creek over several seasons',2]])]],
['Measurement methods',[
'Use simple, reliable methods: **sign-in sheets** and **head counts** for attendance; **pre- and post-surveys or quizzes** for knowledge; **pledge cards** and **sign-up counts** for behavior intentions; **partner data** (take-back pounds collected, donor registrations); **observation** (litter counts before and after a clean-up); and **analytics** for digital reach.',
'Keep surveys short, anonymous and approved by partners or your school. Collect a **baseline** before the campaign so you can show change. Record dates and locations for every measurement.',
'Choose measures you can actually collect—simple and complete beats complicated and missing.',
"Decide in advance who collects each measure at each event. A designated counter at the entrance, a teammate handing out quizzes and another taking photos prevents gaps in your evidence.",
match('Objective and measure',[['Increase knowledge of stroke signs','Pre/post quiz'],['Increase organ donor registrations','Registration count from partner'],['Reach students','Morning announcement enrollment estimate'],['Encourage safe disposal','Pounds of medication collected'],['Reduce creek litter','Before-and-after litter counts'],['Grow online awareness','Views and shares from analytics']])]],
['Calculating and presenting results',[
'Turn data into clear results. Report **counts** (“112 families attended”), **percentages** (“68% of participants could name three stroke signs, up from 22%”) and **comparisons to goals** (“We exceeded our goal of 60%”). Calculate percentages carefully: number achieving the result ÷ number surveyed × 100.',
'Present results in simple **tables** or **charts** in the portfolio, with sources and dates. In the presentation, highlight two or three key numbers that show accomplishment.',
'Be honest about limitations, such as small survey samples or estimates, and about objectives you did not meet—these feed your areas for improvement.',
"Charts can make results easy to understand: a simple bar graph comparing pre- and post-quiz scores or a table of audience by media source fits well on one portfolio page. Label axes, include dates and keep designs clean.",
ex('Calculating a change','In an invented workshop series, 15 of 75 participants named three stroke signs before, and 54 of 75 after.',['Before: 15 ÷ 75 = 0.20 = 20%','After: 54 ÷ 75 = 0.72 = 72%','Change: 72% − 20% = 52 percentage points','Compare to goal: goal was 70%, so it was exceeded'],'Knowledge rose from 20% to 72%, exceeding the 70% goal (data invented for practice).')]],
['Dated evidence of community participation',[
'The rubric rewards **four or more forms of evidence**—such as **dated programs, photos, sign-in sheets, partner letters, screenshots and newspaper clippings**—showing widespread community participation, with **published dates** and **estimated audience numbers** supported by evidence.',
'Photograph every activity, showing participants (with permission) and team members in action. Save programs and agendas with dates. Ask partners for short letters or emails confirming participation and numbers.',
'Organize evidence in folders by activity, labeled with dates, so building the portfolio is straightforward.',
"If an activity is reported in the media, save the article or post with its date as additional evidence. Partner newsletters and school announcements also count when they show dates and audiences.",
order('Evidence folder for each activity',['Activity plan and date','Publicity copies with dates','Sign-in sheets and counts','Dated photos with captions','Survey results or partner data','Partner confirmation email'])]],
['Evidence of every team member’s participation',[
'Judges score **evidence of all team members’ quality participation**. Make sure each member appears in dated photos, programs or videos presenting, teaching, staffing booths or leading activities—not only working behind the scenes.',
'Keep a participation log: who did what, when. Include captions in the portfolio that name members and their roles (“Ana and Jordan lead a hands-only CPR demonstration, February 8”). Programs listing student presenters are excellent verification.',
'Balanced participation also prepares every member to speak confidently in the Round Two presentation.',
"Ask partners to sign a short letter on letterhead confirming the activities you did together, the dates and the number of people involved. Partner confirmation is strong, credible evidence that judges trust.",
chk('Which evidence best shows a team member’s quality participation?',['A dated photo captioned with the member leading a workshop','A list of members’ names only','A photo of an empty room','A note saying “everyone helped”'],'Show members in action, with dates.')]],
['Ethics in data collection',[
'Collect only the data you need, keep surveys **anonymous** when possible and store information securely. Get **permission** before photographing people, especially minors and patients, and follow partner and school policies.',
'Never **fabricate** or **inflate** numbers. Judges and partners may check, and misleading data damages trust. If a number is an estimate, say so and explain how you calculated it.',
'Ethical data practices model the professionalism expected of future health professionals.',
"If something goes wrong—a survey with too few responses or an event with low turnout—record it honestly. These details support your areas for improvement and show integrity.",
sort('Ethical data practice?',['Ethical','Unethical'],[['Anonymous surveys approved by the school',0],['Rounding 40 attendees up to 400',1],['Labeling estimates as estimates',0],['Posting photos of patients without consent',1],['Storing sign-in sheets securely',0]])]],
['Practice scenario',[
kase('Proving impact',{a:{text:'Your goal was for 10% of 1,000 students to register as organ donors. The partner reports 120 new student registrations. (Scenario invented for practice.) Did you meet the goal?',choices:[['Yes—120 is 12%, exceeding the 10% goal','b','Correct.',true],['No—you needed 1,000','b','10% of 1,000 is 100.',false]]},b:{text:'How do you present this?',choices:[['State the goal, the result with the partner as the source and the date range','c','Correct.',true],['Just say “It went great”','c','Specific evidence earns points.',false]]},c:{text:'You add the partner’s confirmation email and a dated photo of the sign-up table to the portfolio.',end:true}})]]
],takeaways:['Outputs count activity (events, attendance, views), outcomes show change (knowledge, sign-ups, behavior) and impact is long-term change; match each objective to a measure before starting.','Use sign-ins, pre/post quizzes, pledge and registration counts, partner data, observation and analytics; collect a baseline; calculate percentages carefully and compare results to goals honestly.','Provide four or more dated forms of evidence with realistic audience numbers, show every member participating in captioned photos and programs and follow ethical data practices—anonymous, consented and never inflated.'],terms:[['Output','A count of campaign activity, such as people reached.'],['Outcome','A change in the audience’s knowledge, attitudes or behavior.'],['Pre/post survey','A survey given before and after an activity to measure change.']],mcqs:[q('Which is an outcome?','80 students signed up as organ donors','300 flyers distributed','5 events held','A logo design','Behavior change.'),q('Which is an output?','150 people attended workshops','Quiz scores improved','Fewer poisonings over years','More people chose water','Counts activity.'),q('A pre/post quiz measures:','Change in knowledge','Budget','Number of partners','Social media followers only','Before and after.'),q('In an invented example, 54 of 75 participants answered correctly. What percentage is that?','72%','54%','75%','20%','54 ÷ 75.'),q('How many forms of dated evidence earn the top impact score?','Four or more','One','Two','Ten','Widespread participation.'),q('Which best shows team member participation?','A dated, captioned photo of a member leading an activity','A list of names','A blank program','A note saying everyone helped','Show action.'),q('Which is unethical?','Inflating attendance numbers','Labeling estimates','Anonymous surveys','Getting photo consent','Honesty.'),q('Why collect a baseline?','To show change after the campaign','To replace activities','To skip surveys','To fill pages','Comparison.')],frq:{prompt:'Explain how your team would measure and document the impact of a Community Awareness campaign, including outputs, outcomes, evidence and ethics.',rubric:[['Outputs and outcomes',['outputs','attendance','views','outcomes','knowledge','behavior','impact']],['Methods',['sign-in','pre','post','survey','baseline','partner data','analytics']],['Evidence',['four','dated','photos','programs','captions','every member']],['Ethics and reporting',['anonymous','consent','estimate','inflate','percent','goal','honest']]],model:'Before our campaign on safe medication disposal began, we matched each objective to a measure. Outputs would include the number of booths and workshops held, attendance from sign-in sheets, brochures distributed and video views from analytics. Outcomes would include knowledge measured by a short anonymous pre- and post-quiz approved by our school and pharmacy partner, pledges signed and, most importantly, behavior: the pounds of medication collected at take-back day, reported by the pharmacy, compared with last year’s total as a baseline. Long-term impact, such as fewer accidental poisonings, would be described as the expected benefit. We would calculate results carefully—for example, if 48 of 60 participants identified safe disposal methods after a workshop compared with 18 of 60 before, knowledge rose from 30% to 80%, exceeding our 70% goal—and present them in a simple table with dates. For evidence, we would keep a folder for each activity with the plan, dated publicity, sign-in sheets, captioned photos showing each team member presenting or staffing booths, survey results and a partner confirmation email, aiming for at least four forms of dated evidence with realistic audience numbers. Ethically, we would keep surveys anonymous, get permission before photographing anyone, store sign-in sheets securely, label estimates clearly and never inflate numbers, honestly noting any objective we did not meet as an area for improvement.'},sources:[RULES,CDC_EVAL,CTB_EVAL]};

export const CA8={title:'Building the portfolio',hook:'Judges score your portfolio before they meet you, using a detailed rubric worth 80 points. They are looking for a complete, organized story of your campaign within 12 pages: what you did, how you publicized it, who participated and what changed. A clean, well-formatted portfolio that follows every rule earns points before a single word is spoken.',objectives:['Include every required portfolio component in a logical order.','Apply the narrative formatting rules exactly.','Present activities, publicity, participation and impact persuasively.','Upload a single PDF correctly and on time.'],sections:[
['Required components',[
'The portfolio (up to **12 pages**, not counting reference pages) must include: **(a)** a **title page**—event name, team member names, HOSA division, chapter number, school name, chartered association, **title of campaign** and **target audience**, centered, one page; **(b)** **activities conducted**, including a **timeline**, plus original items such as publication links, pamphlets, photos, social media posts, webinars or podcasts; **(c)** **publicity/marketing** with brief explanations; **(d)** **verification of competitors presenting**—dated programs or pictures with explanations; **(e)** **people impacted**—documentation of the number of people reached, with realistic estimates; and **(g)** **references**.',
'Organize these in a clear order so judges can find each item quickly. Headings that match the rubric language help.',
'Remember that the campaign must focus on a **health or safety issue** of local, state or national interest—state the issue clearly near the start.',
"Write a brief campaign overview paragraph at the start that states the issue, why it matters locally and your goal. It gives judges context before they review the details.",
order('Suggested portfolio order',['Title page','Campaign overview: issue, goal and audience','Activities conducted with timeline','Original items','Publicity and marketing','Verification of members presenting','People impacted','References'])]],
['Formatting rules',[
'All **narrative pages** must be **one-sided**, **typed**, in **12-point Arial**, **double-spaced**, on **8½ × 11-inch paper** with **1-inch margins**, **numbered on the top right** (not counting the title page) and with a **running header** of **team members’ last names and the event name** on the top left (not counting the title page). Formatting is scored all-or-nothing, so check every narrative page.',
'Set up a template before writing so all members’ sections match. Pages with photos and items can use captions, but narrative text must follow the rules.',
'Keep the portfolio within **12 pages**; reference pages do not count toward the limit.',
flip('Formatting checklist',[['Font','12-point Arial'],['Spacing','Double-spaced'],['Margins','1 inch'],['Page numbers','Top right, title page not counted'],['Running header','Last names and event name, top left'],['Length','12 pages maximum plus references']])]],
['Presenting activities and items',[
'The **activities** section should show a **timeline** of everything you did, with dates, locations, partners and short descriptions. Judges reward **exceptional, original activities** that highlight research and a **call to action**. Explain why each activity was chosen and how it connected to your goal.',
'Show **original items**—brochures, posters, videos, posts, podcasts—with images, links and a sentence on purpose and use. The top score requires **four or more** high-quality items. Make sure every link is set to **public** so judges can open it.',
'A simple timeline table (date, activity, partner, audience) is an efficient way to present many activities in little space.',
"Choose your strongest photos: clear, well-lit images showing team members interacting with the community. Crop out clutter, and include captions with the date, activity and members pictured.",
{t:'table',caption:'Sample activity timeline (invented for practice)',head:['Date','Activity','Partner','Audience'],rows:[['Oct 12','Kick-off assembly presentation','School administration','1,100 students'],['Nov 9','Library workshop','Public library','42 adults'],['Jan 18','Health fair booth','Hospital','260 visitors'],['Mar 14','Take-back day','Pharmacy','95 families']]}]],
['Publicity, participation and impact pages',[
'The **publicity** section should show each media source with a copy or screenshot, the date and a brief explanation, aiming for **four or more forms of media** and realistic audience estimates. The **verification** section shows **dated programs and photos** of team members presenting or participating, with captions naming members. The **people impacted** section totals the audience by source, explains each estimate and highlights outcomes such as sign-ups or survey results.',
'Use clear headings, captions and short explanations—judges should understand each page in seconds.',
'Evidence that is **dated** and **specific** is far more convincing than general claims.',
"Use a summary table at the start of the people impacted section that lists each activity and media source with its audience and evidence, followed by a total. Judges appreciate seeing the full reach at a glance.",
sort('Belongs in which section?',['Publicity','Verification of members','People impacted'],[['Newspaper article with date',0],['Photo of two members teaching a workshop',1],['Table of audience totals by source',2],['Radio interview link',0],['Program listing student presenters',1],['Survey results showing knowledge gain',2]])]],
['References and quality',[
'List **all literature cited** on reference pages; **APA** is preferred. Formatting of references is not judged, but a **clean, legible** reference page earns points. Every fact in your materials should be traceable to a reference.',
'The rubric also scores **spelling, grammar, punctuation and neatness**: a portfolio with **no errors** that is very neat earns full points. Proofread every page, check names and dates and ask someone outside the team to review it.',
'Consistent design—colors, fonts, headings—makes the portfolio look professional.',
chk('Do reference pages count toward the 12-page limit?',['No','Yes','Only the first one','Only if they are long'],'References are excluded from the 12-page maximum.')]],
['Uploading the PDF',[
'**One team member** uploads the portfolio as **one combined PDF** to the HOSA Digital Upload System by **May 15 at midnight Eastern time**—no exceptions. If it is not a single PDF, judges will not view it and no points are awarded; inaccessible links or documents also earn nothing. If your state used the upload system, you must upload again for ILC.',
'Before uploading, export to PDF, open the file to check formatting and page count, click every link in a private browser and confirm the file name is clear. Upload several days early and save the confirmation.',
'Teams that do not upload cannot advance to the Round Two presentation.',
order('Upload checklist',['Export one combined PDF','Check page count and formatting','Test every link publicly','Proofread one final time','Upload several days before May 15','Save the confirmation'])]],
['Practice scenario',[
kase('Portfolio review',{a:{text:'Your draft portfolio is 15 pages plus 2 reference pages. (Scenario invented for practice.) What must change?',choices:[['Cut to 12 pages; references do not count','b','Correct.',true],['Nothing; references make it 17 anyway','b','The limit is 12 pages plus references.',false]]},b:{text:'One teammate’s pages use 11-point Times New Roman. What do you do?',choices:[['Reformat to 12-point Arial, double-spaced, with header and page numbers','c','Correct. Formatting is all-or-nothing.',true],['Leave it','c','Formatting must match on every narrative page.',false]]},c:{text:'Your final portfolio is 12 pages, correctly formatted, with every link tested.',end:true}})]]
],takeaways:['Include a centered one-page title page (event, names, division, chapter number, school, chartered association, campaign title, target audience), activities with timeline and original items, publicity, verification of members presenting, people impacted and references.','Narrative pages: one-sided, typed, 12-pt Arial, double-spaced, 1-inch margins, page numbers top right and running header (last names, event) top left; 12 pages maximum not counting references.','Aim for four or more original items and media sources with dated, captioned evidence; proofread for zero errors; upload one PDF with public links by May 15 and save the confirmation.'],terms:[['Portfolio','The pre-judged document showing the campaign and its results.'],['Running header','Text at the top of each page with names and event.'],['Timeline','A dated list of campaign activities.']],mcqs:[q('What is the portfolio page limit?','12 pages, not counting references','20 pages including references','6 pages','No limit','Pre-judged.'),q('Which font is required for narrative pages?','12-point Arial','10-point Times New Roman','14-point Comic Sans','Any font','Formatting rule.'),q('What must the title page include besides team information?','Title of campaign and target audience','A budget','A photo of judges','A reference list','Required items.'),q('Where do page numbers go?','Top right','Bottom center','Top left','Nowhere','Not counting title page.'),q('How is the portfolio submitted?','As one combined PDF by May 15','As separate files','By mail','At ILC on paper','Digital upload.'),q('Which reference style is preferred?','APA','MLA only','None','Chicago only','Health science.'),q('What happens if links are not accessible to judges?','No points for those items','Judges request access','Nothing','Extra time','Test links.'),q('How many errors earn full spelling and grammar points?','None','A few','Five','Ten','Error-free.')],frq:{prompt:'Describe how your team would organize, format and submit a Community Awareness portfolio to earn full points.',rubric:[['Components',['title page','activities','timeline','original items','publicity','verification','people impacted','references']],['Formatting',['12','arial','double','1-inch','page number','running header','12 pages']],['Quality',['four','dated','captions','public links','proofread','apa']],['Submission',['one pdf','may 15','one member','test','confirmation']]],model:'We would organize our portfolio in a clear order within 12 pages, not counting references. Page 1 is a centered title page listing the event name, team member names, HOSA division, chapter number, school name, chartered association, our campaign title and target audience. Next comes a short overview stating the health issue, our research-based purpose and our goal. The activities section presents a dated timeline table of every activity with partners and audiences, followed by images and public links to at least four original items—a brochure, a PSA video, an infographic and pledge cards—each with a sentence on its purpose. The publicity section shows copies or screenshots of four or more media sources, such as a newspaper article, a radio interview, partner newsletters and social media posts, each dated with a realistic audience estimate. The verification section includes dated programs and captioned photos showing every team member presenting or leading activities. The people impacted section totals the audience by source, explains each estimate and highlights outcomes such as survey gains and take-back totals. Reference pages list all sources in APA style. Every narrative page is typed, one-sided, 12-point Arial, double-spaced, with one-inch margins, page numbers at the top right and a running header with our last names and the event name at the top left. We would proofread for zero errors, export one combined PDF, test every link in a private browser and have one member upload it several days before May 15, saving the confirmation.'},sources:[RULES,UPLOADS,APA]};

export const CA9={title:'The five-minute presentation',hook:'Five minutes, no slides and no visual aids—just your team telling the story of a year of work. Judges score six content items worth 60 points, so every sentence must earn its place. A clear structure that covers each required element, backed by specific numbers and partner examples, lets your campaign speak for itself.',objectives:['List the six required presentation elements and how they are scored.','Build a five-minute outline that covers every element.','Use specific evidence and examples convincingly.','Follow the rules on notecards and team participation.'],sections:[
['Required content',[
'The presentation must include: **(a)** the **purpose** for selecting the campaign with a brief summary of its development; **(b)** the **research** used in selecting and developing it; **(c)** a description of **local community partnerships**; **(d)** the **goal** and **activities** used to promote and complete the campaign; **(e)** **evidence of accomplishment** of goals and objectives; and **(f)** the **impact** of the campaign and **areas for improvement**.',
'Judges score six content items, each worth **10 points**: purpose for selection and development, research and understanding of the problem, activities conducted, objectives and accomplishments, impact (including a positive call to action) and cooperative work with local community partners (four or more examples for the top score). Delivery items are scored as well.',
'Because the portfolio was already scored, the presentation should **highlight** and **explain**, not repeat every detail.',
"Think of the presentation as a story with a beginning (the problem and why you cared), a middle (what you did and with whom) and an end (what changed and what you learned). A story is easier for judges to follow and remember than a list of facts.",
match('Required element and what to say',[['Purpose','Why your team chose this issue'],['Research','Key data showing the problem and its causes'],['Partnerships','Who you worked with and their impact'],['Goal and activities','Your SMART goal and main activities'],['Accomplishment','Results compared with your goal'],['Impact and improvement','Community change and what you would do differently']])]],
['A five-minute outline',[
'A sample plan: **Opening and purpose (0:00–0:45)**—a hook and why you chose the issue. **Research (0:45–1:30)**—two or three key facts, including local data and your needs assessment. **Goal and activities (1:30–2:45)**—your SMART goal and the most important activities with dates. **Partnerships (2:45–3:30)**—four concrete examples of partner impact. **Accomplishments and impact (3:30–4:30)**—results compared with goals and the call to action. **Improvement and close (4:30–5:00)**—areas for improvement and a memorable final line.',
'The timekeeper announces **one minute remaining** and stops the presentation at **five minutes**. Rehearse to finish around **4:45**.',
'Prioritize the elements worth the most points and the strongest evidence; cut anything that does not serve a required element.',
"Assign each section to a member based on who led that part of the campaign. Speakers who personally ran an activity describe it with more detail and confidence.",
order('Five-minute outline',['Hook and purpose','Research highlights','Goal and key activities','Four partnership examples','Accomplishments and impact','Areas for improvement and close'])]],
['Showing research and understanding',[
'The research item rewards **in-depth** research and a **deep, insightful understanding** of the problem. Go beyond the obvious: give a **local** statistic, explain a **cause** and name a **solution** your campaign used. For example: “County data show our youth vaping rate is above the state average; our survey found 60% of students did not know vapes can harm brain development, so our campaign focused on brain health.”',
'Cite sources orally (“according to our state health department’s 2025 report”). Keep numbers few and memorable.',
'Show how research **shaped** your choices—that is the evidence judges want.',
"Avoid reading statistics as a list. Pick the single most striking local number and explain what it means for real people in your community.",
chk('Which statement shows deep understanding of the problem?',['A local statistic, a cause identified by your survey and how your campaign addressed it','“Vaping is bad.”','A long list of national numbers with no explanation','“Everyone knows this is a problem.”'],'Connect research to campaign choices.')]],
['Showing accomplishment and impact',[
'For **accomplishments**, state each main objective and the result: “Our goal was 10% of students registering as organ donors; our partner reported 12%.” For **impact**, explain what changed in the community and the **call to action** you inspired: pledges, sign-ups, new habits, lasting resources.',
'Highlight **lasting change**: a permanent display, a new school policy, a partner adopting your program. These show impact beyond the campaign year.',
'Honest numbers delivered confidently are more persuasive than vague superlatives.',
"End your accomplishments section with the clearest call to action your campaign used, such as “Register as a donor today,” so judges hear the campaign’s message in your own voices.",
sort('Strong or weak impact statement?',['Strong','Weak'],[['“120 students registered, exceeding our 10% goal”',0],['“It was really successful”',1],['“The pharmacy now keeps our display permanently”',0],['“Lots of people liked it”',1],['“Knowledge rose from 20% to 72%”',0]])]],
['Areas for improvement',[
'The presentation must include **areas for improvement**. Choose one or two genuine lessons: “Our first workshop had low attendance because it conflicted with a school event; we learned to check community calendars.” “We would add a Spanish-language version of our video earlier to reach more families.”',
'Present improvements as **growth**, not failure. Judges respect teams that reflect honestly and show they would make the next campaign stronger.',
'Connect improvements to next steps, such as continuing the campaign next year.',
order('Describing an area for improvement',['Name the challenge','Explain why it happened','Describe what you changed or learned','Explain how it would improve the next campaign'])]],
['Notecards and team roles',[
'**Index cards** are allowed; **electronic notecards** are allowed but **may not be shown** to judges. **No additional materials or visual aids**—not the portfolio, posters or props. Use cards for key words and numbers, not full scripts, so you can make eye contact.',
'**All team members must take an active role**. Divide sections so speaking time is balanced: for a team of four, each member might cover one or two elements. Plan smooth transitions: “Now Malik will share how our partners helped us reach more families.”',
'Rehearse handoffs until the presentation flows as one story.',
"Write each speaker’s key numbers in large print on their cards so they can glance down quickly without losing eye contact. Practice with the exact cards you will use at competition.",
sort('Allowed in the presentation?',['Allowed','Not allowed'],[['Paper index cards',0],['Holding up the portfolio',1],['Electronic notes not shown to judges',0],['A poster of photos',1],['A slideshow',1]])]],
['Practice scenario',[
kase('Timing problem',{a:{text:'In rehearsal, your team spends 2 minutes on purpose and runs out of time before impact. (Scenario invented for practice.) What do you change?',choices:[['Shorten purpose to about 45 seconds so impact and improvement get full time','b','Correct.',true],['Speak faster everywhere','b','Rebalance instead.',false]]},b:{text:'Your partnership section names only one partner. What do you add?',choices:[['Three more concrete partner impact examples','c','Correct. Four or more earns the top score.',true],['Nothing; one is enough','c','Four or more examples score highest.',false]]},c:{text:'Your next rehearsal covers all six elements and ends at 4:45.',end:true}})]]
],takeaways:['Required: purpose and development, research, community partnerships, goal and activities, evidence of accomplishment and impact with areas for improvement; six content items worth 10 points each, plus delivery.','Outline five minutes (purpose, research, goal and activities, four partner examples, accomplishments and impact, improvement and close) finishing near 4:45; cite local research and show how it shaped choices.','State results against goals, highlight lasting change and honest areas for improvement; notecards only (electronic notes not shown), no visual aids, every member speaks with smooth transitions.'],terms:[['Area for improvement','A lesson learned that would strengthen a future campaign.'],['Accomplishment','A result showing progress toward a goal.'],['Transition','A phrase that hands off smoothly to the next speaker.']],mcqs:[q('How long is the presentation?','5 minutes','10 minutes','2 minutes','15 minutes','One-minute warning.'),q('Which is a required presentation element?','Areas for improvement','The team’s favorite food','A slideshow','The budget only','Element f.'),q('How many points is each content item worth?','10','5','20','15','Six items.'),q('Which visual aid is allowed?','None—only notecards','A poster','The portfolio','A slideshow','No visual aids.'),q('How many partner examples earn the top cooperative work score?','Four or more','One','Two','Three','Strong evidence.'),q('Which statement shows accomplishment?','“120 students registered, exceeding our 10% goal”','“It went well”','“People liked it”','“We tried hard”','Specific.'),q('Who must take an active role?','All team members','Only the captain','Two members','No one','Participation.'),q('Electronic notecards:','Are allowed but may not be shown to judges','Must be shown to judges','Are not allowed','Replace speaking','GRR rule.')],frq:{prompt:'Write a five-minute presentation outline for an invented Community Awareness campaign that covers all six required elements.',rubric:[['Purpose and research',['purpose','why','research','local','data','survey']],['Goal, activities and partners',['goal','smart','activities','partners','four','examples']],['Accomplishment and impact',['result','exceeded','percent','impact','call to action','lasting']],['Improvement and rules',['improvement','learned','notecards','every member','4:45']]],model:'Our invented campaign, “Know the Signs, Our Town,” helped older adults recognize stroke warning signs. From 0:00 to 0:45, Ana opens with a short story about a neighbor whose stroke went unrecognized and explains that we chose this issue after our hospital partner told us many local stroke patients arrive too late for treatment. From 0:45 to 1:30, Ben shares research: our county’s stroke death rate is above the state average, and our needs assessment found only 20% of senior center members could name three warning signs, so we focused on BE FAST and calling 911. From 1:30 to 2:45, Carla states our SMART goal—raising that figure to 70% between November and March—and describes our main activities: four workshops, bilingual large-print flyers and a radio interview. From 2:45 to 3:30, Dev gives four partnership examples: the hospital stroke coordinator reviewed every message and co-taught two workshops; two senior centers hosted sessions for 120 members; the radio station interviewed us; and the pharmacy agreed to keep our stroke-sign display permanently. From 3:30 to 4:30, Ana reports results: knowledge rose from 20% to 72%, exceeding our goal, and 58 participants posted our 911 magnet at home. From 4:30 to 4:45, Ben describes an area for improvement—our first workshop conflicted with a community event, so we now check calendars—and closes with our call to action. We use index cards only, every member speaks and we rehearse to finish at 4:45.'},sources:[RULES,CTB]};

export const CA10={title:'Delivery, reflection and sustaining the campaign',hook:'A year of community work deserves a confident, polished presentation. Delivery points reward clear voices, strong presence and true teamwork—and judges notice teams who speak with pride and humility about what they achieved. Afterward, the best campaigns do not simply end: they become traditions that keep improving community health.',objectives:['Apply delivery criteria for voice, stage presence, diction and team participation.','Rehearse effectively and manage nerves.','Reflect honestly on the campaign’s strengths and weaknesses.','Plan to sustain the campaign and connect it to health careers.'],sections:[
['Voice and diction',[
'Speak **loudly and clearly** enough for judges to hear easily, **vary** your rate and volume to emphasize key points and **pause** before important numbers. Avoid **vocal fillers** such as “um,” “uh” and “like,” and pronounce health terms correctly.',
'Your tone should match your message: warm and enthusiastic about your community, serious when describing the health problem, confident when reporting results.',
'Record a rehearsal and listen for rushing, fillers and moments where energy drops.',
"Practice difficult words and numbers aloud until they are automatic. Stumbling over a key statistic undercuts confidence, while delivering it smoothly makes it memorable.",
"Before the presentation, warm up your voice by reading your opening lines aloud a few times. A strong start sets a confident tone for the whole team.",
sort('Strong or weak delivery?',['Strong','Weak'],[['Pausing before a key result',0],['Mumbling into notecards',1],['Varied volume for emphasis',0],['Constant “um” and “like”',1],['Clear pronunciation of health terms',0],['Speaking in a monotone',1]])]],
['Stage presence and teamwork',[
'**Stage presence** includes poise, posture, eye contact and enthusiasm. Stand tall, face the judges, make eye contact with each one and use natural gestures. Teammates who are not speaking should stay engaged, looking at the speaker and reacting naturally.',
'**Team participation** rewards shared collaboration—every member speaking and showing knowledge of the campaign. Balanced speaking parts and smooth handoffs show judges that the whole team owned the work.',
'Practice standing positions and transitions so the team moves as a unit.',
"Decide where each member will stand and how you will rotate to the front if needed. Small details such as not blocking a teammate and turning toward each new speaker make the team look polished.",
match('Delivery element and what judges look for',[['Voice','Volume, varied rate, pauses'],['Diction','Clear words and no fillers'],['Stage presence','Posture, eye contact, enthusiasm'],['Team participation','Every member speaks and contributes'],['Transitions','Smooth handoffs between speakers'],['Tone','Sincere and confident']])]],
['Rehearsing and managing nerves',[
'Rehearse the full presentation at least **five times** with a timer, then present to an audience—your advisor, partners or another chapter—and ask for feedback using the rating sheet. Practice in business attire at least once.',
'Manage nerves with slow breathing, a quick team huddle and a reminder of what you accomplished. If someone forgets a line, a teammate can continue smoothly—another reason everyone should know the whole story.',
'Confidence comes from preparation and from knowing your campaign deeply.',
"Ask practice judges for specific feedback, such as “Which part felt rushed?” or “Was our impact clear?” General praise is pleasant, but specific feedback helps you improve.",
order('Rehearsal plan',['Write and time the outline','Practice individual sections','Run the full presentation with transitions','Present to an audience for feedback','Revise and rehearse to about 4:45','Final run in business attire'])]],
['Reflecting on the campaign',[
'Honest reflection improves both your presentation and future campaigns. As a team, discuss: Which activities had the most impact? Which partners were most valuable? What surprised you? What would you change? Which objectives were met, and which were not, and why?',
'Use a simple **after-action review** for the whole campaign: what was planned, what happened, what went well and what to improve. Choose one or two meaningful areas for improvement to share with judges.',
'Reflection also helps each member describe the experience in college and scholarship applications.',
"Write down the reflection in a short document. It helps you prepare the improvement section of the presentation, gives next year’s team a starting point and provides material for applications and interviews.",
order('Campaign reflection questions',['What did we plan?','What actually happened?','What went well?','What would we improve?','What did each of us learn?'])]],
['Sustaining the campaign',[
'Many successful campaigns continue for years. Hand off the campaign to **younger members** with a guide that includes partner contacts, materials, timelines and lessons learned. Keep partners engaged with thank-you notes and results summaries, and invite them to continue next year.',
'Look for **lasting changes** your campaign can leave behind: a permanent display, a school policy, a partner program, an annual event or online resources that stay available. These extend impact beyond the competition.',
'Sustained campaigns also strengthen your chapter’s reputation and recruitment.',
"Share final results with your community: a thank-you post, a short article in the school newspaper or a presentation to your school board. Celebrating success encourages continued participation and recognizes everyone who helped.",
"Consider expanding next year’s campaign to a new audience, such as a feeder middle school or a community center in another neighborhood, using the same proven activities.",
sort('Sustains the campaign?',['Sustains impact','Ends impact'],[['A handoff guide for next year’s members',0],['Deleting all materials after competition',1],['An annual take-back event with the pharmacy',0],['Losing partner contacts',1],['A permanent library display',0],['A school policy adding water stations',0]])]],
['Connecting to health careers',[
'Community Awareness builds skills used by **health education specialists**, **community health workers**, **public health nurses**, **health communication specialists** and **epidemiologists**. Health education specialists, for example, plan and evaluate programs that teach people about health—exactly what your team did.',
'Skills you practiced—research, planning, partnership-building, communication, evaluation and teamwork—are valued in every health career. Note them in résumés and applications with specific results.',
'Ask partners about their careers; many are happy to mentor students interested in public health.',
"Keep a portfolio of your own contributions—photos, items you designed and results—to use when applying for programs, scholarships and jobs. Concrete examples of community health work stand out to reviewers.",
match('Career and connection to the campaign',[['Health education specialist','Plans and evaluates health programs'],['Community health worker','Connects people to services'],['Health communication specialist','Designs health messages'],['Public health nurse','Educates communities and provides care'],['Epidemiologist','Uses data to understand health problems'],['Nonprofit program coordinator','Manages community partnerships']])]],
['Practice scenario',[
kase('After the campaign',{a:{text:'Your seniors are graduating, and the campaign succeeded. (Scenario invented for practice.) What is the best next step?',choices:[['Write a handoff guide and introduce younger members to partners','b','Correct.',true],['Let the campaign end','b','Sustain the impact.',false]]},b:{text:'In the presentation, how do you describe a weak activity?',choices:[['As an area for improvement with what you learned','c','Correct.',true],['Hide it','c','Honest reflection earns respect.',false]]},c:{text:'Your team presents confidently and plans to continue the campaign next year.',end:true}})]]
],takeaways:['Delivery: loud, clear voices with varied rate and pauses, no fillers; poise, eye contact and enthusiasm; every member speaking with smooth transitions.','Rehearse at least five times with a timer and an audience; manage nerves with breathing and teamwork; reflect honestly with an after-action review and choose real areas for improvement.','Sustain the campaign with handoff guides, partner relationships and lasting changes; connect the experience to careers such as health education specialist and community health worker.'],terms:[['Stage presence','Poise, posture, eye contact and enthusiasm while presenting.'],['After-action review','A structured reflection on what went well and what to improve.'],['Sustainability','Keeping a campaign’s impact going over time.']],mcqs:[q('Which is a vocal filler?','“Um”','A pause','A statistic','A transition','Avoid fillers.'),q('Which shows strong stage presence?','Eye contact with each judge','Reading notes with head down','Fidgeting','Turning away','Poise.'),q('Team participation is strongest when:','Every member speaks and contributes','One member speaks','Members stay silent','Only the captain answers','Shared collaboration.'),q('How many full rehearsals are recommended?','At least five','One','None','Fifty','Practice.'),q('Which sustains a campaign?','A handoff guide for next year','Deleting materials','Losing partners','Ending all activities','Continuity.'),q('Which career plans and evaluates health programs?','Health education specialist','Radiologist','Pharmacist','Surgeon','Direct connection.'),q('An after-action review asks:','What went well and what to improve','Who is to blame','How to hide mistakes','Nothing','Reflection.'),q('Which is a lasting change?','A permanent library display','A single tweet','A one-day booth only','A thank-you card','Beyond the campaign.')],frq:{prompt:'Explain how your team will deliver a strong presentation, reflect on the campaign and sustain its impact after the competition.',rubric:[['Delivery',['voice','volume','pause','fillers','eye contact','posture','enthusiasm']],['Teamwork and rehearsal',['every member','transitions','rehearse','timer','feedback','nerves']],['Reflection',['after-action','went well','improve','objectives','honest']],['Sustainability',['handoff','younger members','partners','lasting','annual','careers']]],model:'To deliver strongly, each of our four members will speak for about a minute, with planned transitions that hand off the story smoothly. We will speak loudly and clearly, pause before key results such as our 72% knowledge score and avoid fillers by recording rehearsals and practicing problem spots. We will stand tall, make eye contact with each judge and show genuine enthusiasm for our community, while teammates who are not speaking stay engaged. We will rehearse at least five times with a timer, present to our advisor and hospital partner for feedback using the rating sheet and do a final run in business attire, using slow breathing and a team huddle to manage nerves. For reflection, we will hold an after-action review comparing what we planned with what happened: our workshops and pharmacy partnership had the most impact, while our first event had low attendance because it conflicted with a community festival. We will present that honestly as an area for improvement and explain that we now check community calendars. To sustain the campaign, we will write a handoff guide for younger members with partner contacts, materials, timelines and lessons, introduce them to our partners and plan an annual “Know the Signs” workshop series with the senior centers, building on the pharmacy’s permanent display. The experience also prepares us for careers such as health education specialist and community health worker.'},sources:[RULES,BLS_HE,CTB]};
